Peter Petrakis wrote:
> > If the package is already compiled, is there any way
> > to jump past the prep, compile, and install stages straight to building a
> > binary?
> 
> 
> That's a good question. After looking at the man page it appears that
> there no way
> to get around that.

Absolutely on purpose; in order to prevent sneaking in a weird stuff
and manual interventions when making final archives.

> I would suck having to rebuild all those object
> files. Might
> we benfit by hacking the rpm source? :-)

It is possible to create and use another spec file which will be doing
mostly nothing until an installation phase.  Like one for Netscape from
DU. :-) Techically this will be a different rpm archive then the one you
tried to build in the first place.  Either redefine prep to do mostly
nothing or prepare "sources" from your current compilation tree.
Do not unpack on the top what you compiled or you will be starting
from scratch again.  The first action in a normal prep is 'rm -rf ...'.

  Michal




